  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \nThe proposed approach combines random walk exploration, frequency domain optimization, and adaptive dimensionality reduction to solve the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. Unlike previous methods that treated the step function directly in the spatial domain, this strategy works in the spectral domain, which has the following advantages:  \n\n- **Improved Convexification**: Mapping the step function to frequency domain coefficients transforms many terms into convex combinations due to Parseval's theorem, allowing more stable optimization  \n- **Scale-Invariant Optimization**: Operation in coefficient space bypasses numerical instability issues from spatial discretization  \n- **Adaptive Search**: Dimensions are progressively reduced to focus computational resources on the most influential frequencies  \n\nThe method leverages the observation that optimal solutions often exhibit self-similar properties, which are naturally captured in the frequency domain. The algorithm starts with high-dimensional exploration and systematically reduces complexity, balancing global exploration and local refinement.  \n\nKey limitations:  \n- Performance depends critically on frequency truncation choices  \n- Requires careful initialization to avoid convergence to trivial solutions  \n- Optimization of amplitude coefficients may miss boundary effects apparent in direct space representations  \n\n2. Mathematical specification  \nThe step function \\(h: [0,2] \\to [0,1]\\) is represented via Fourier series:  \n\\(h(x) = \\sum_{k=-N}^{N} C_k e^{2\u03c0i k x / 2}\\)  \n\nwhere \\(C_k\\) are complex coefficients. The following constraints are incorporated:  \n- \\(0 \\leq h(x) \\leq 1\\) via bounds on amplitude spectra  \n- Integral constraint \\(\\int_0^2 h(x)dx=1\\) mapped to a coefficient-level condition  \n- Smoothness maintained via absolute coefficient amplitudes  \n\nThe adaptive dimensionality reduction selects the highest-frequency components whose cumulative energy exceeds a threshold:  \n\\(\\sum_{k=|\\lambda|}\\) until \\(\\sum_{k} |C_k|^2 \\geq global\\_energy \\times threshold\\)  \n\nThe core optimization is formulated in transformed coordinates as:  \n\\[\\min \\max_k \\left| \\sum_{f \\in F} C_f \\times \\overline{F}(k, f) \\right| \\times dx\\]  \n\nfor selected frequency components \\(f\\), where \\(F\\) is the frequency set.  \n\n3. Implementation procedure  \nInitialization:  \n1. Set target frequency density: \\(N\\) truncated to \\(2^{11}=2048\\)  \n2. Initialize coefficients uniformly in amplitude space  \n3. Feasibility projection using spectral scaling  \n4. Compute normalization factor \\(s = \\sum |C_k|^2\\)  \n\nFrequency selection phase:  \n1. Compute power spectrum \\(P_k = |C_k|^2\\)  \n2. Cumulative selection: include frequencies until energy threshold  \n   \\(0.95 \\times \\sum_k |C_k|^2\\)  \n3. Transform to iteration variables  \n\nCore optimization phase:  \n```\nfor max_freq in range(floor(N), ceil(min_freq), -step):\n    select frequencies \\(F\\) with \\(|max_freq|\\)\n    enforce projected gradient descent\n    update coefficients\n    compute objective via FFT-based correlations\n```\n\nFinal dimensionality reduction:  \nApply customized Nesterov smoothing to lower-dimensional projections.  \n\n4. Search variations  \nImplement three amplitude strategies:  \n1. Complex exponentials: Conforms strictly to Fourier transforms  \n2. Prolate spheroidal: Better captures boundedness constraints  \n3. Wavelet-inspired: Handles sharp transitions better  \n\nEach variation introduces different regularization parameters, but maintains the same spectral selection criteria.  \n\n5. Budget estimation  \n- Initial phase (frequency selection): \\(O(100) \\times \\log(N)\\)  \n- Core optimization: \\(O(10000) \\times d \\times \\log(N)\\)  \n- Budget division:  \n  - 30% for frequency selection  \n  - 50% for core optimization  \n  - 20% for final Nesterov smoothing  \n\n6. Return contract  \nCandidates are validated by:  \n1. Computing exact \\(C_5\\) via discrete correlation  \n2. Ensuring \\(0 \\leq h \u2265 0.9\\)  \n3. Verifying normalization \\(\\frac{\\sum h}{n} \\approx \\frac{1}{2}\\)  \n4. Checking spectral stability \\(| \\sum |C_k|^2 - valid |\\)",
